<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ravidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 15th–16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Raidas, Ravidass</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ravidas was an Indian mystic poet-saint of the Bhakti movement who lived in the 15th and 16th centuries, though the details of his life are uncertain. Born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> to parents of the leather-working <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamar">Chamar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, treated as untouchable, he taught the removal of divisions of caste and gender and worshipped God as nirguna, without attributes. Forty-one of his poems are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Adi Granth</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> scripture, and tradition names the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirabai">Mirabai</a> as his disciple. He is the central figure of the Ravidassia religious movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ravidas.jpg" alt="Ravidas" width="320" height="358" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ravidas,_detail_of_a_painting_of_Kabir_with_Ravidas,_Mughal,_1625_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was first compiled by Guru Arjan in 1604 as the Adi Granth, the “first book”, from the hymns of the first five Gurus and of saints of other traditions, among them the weaver-poet Kabir, the Sufi Shaikh Farid, the leatherworker <b>Ravidas</b> and the tailor Namdev.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Others, the sants of northern India such as Kabir and <b>Ravidas</b>, worshipped God without form or attributes, nirguna, whom no image or scripture could contain, in contrast to the saguna devotion to a god with form.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ravidas</b>, a poet-saint born to a leather-working family, preached against caste, and his verses entered the Sikh scripture, the Adi Granth.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li></ul></section>
